  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2}$ is a solution to the equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{\u2033}+x{y}^{\u2032}-4y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)=3{x}^{4}$\n- $y(x)=-{x}^{2}$\n- $y(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x$",
  "worked_solution": "According to the superposition principle, if the function ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c$ is a constant, then $y(x)=c{y}_{1}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=3{x}^{4}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle because $y(x)={x}^{4}$ is not a solution.\n\n- The function $y(x)=-{x}^{2}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=-1.$\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
